<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yuan Haowen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1190">1190</a>–1257</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yuan Haowen was a poet, critic and historian of the Jin dynasty from Xinzhou in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanxi">Shanxi</a>, widely regarded as the greatest poet of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in his age. He served the Jin court and lived through its fall to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>, which he lamented in his verse. Refusing office under the new rulers, he compiled the Zhongzhou ji, an anthology of Jin poetry, and gathered records later used in the official history of the Jin.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yuan-haowen.jpg" alt="Yuan Haowen" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Outlookxp · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%B1%B1%E8%A5%BF%E7%9C%81%E5%BF%BB%E5%B7%9E%E5%B8%82%E5%BF%BB%E5%BA%9C%E5%8D%80%E9%9F%93%E5%B2%A9%E6%9D%91%E5%85%83%E5%A5%BD%E5%95%8F%E5%A2%93%E7%9A%84%E5%85%83%E5%A5%BD%E5%95%8F%E9%8A%85%E5%83%8F.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
